          "type": "accordion",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How to clean the outside of a copper bottle?",
            "icon": "sparkles",
            "use_product_description": false,
            "open_by_default": false,
            "content": "<p>Dip half a lemon in salt and rub the surface, following the hammer marks. Rinse, then dry with a soft cloth. The copper brightens within minutes.</p><p>If you prefer the deeper tone of aged copper, simply skip this step - both looks are normal.</p>"
          }
        },
        "care_dark_green": {
          "type": "accordion",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why is my copper bottle turning dark or green?",
            "icon": "leaf",
            "use_product_description": false,
            "open_by_default": false,
            "content": "<p>Darkening is normal. Bare copper reacts with air and water and slowly forms a darker patina, inside and out. It is the sign of bare copper, not damage, and a lemon-and-salt clean brings the shine back.</p><p>Green or blue spots form when acid or trapped moisture sits on the copper, for example juice or lemon water in the bottle, or putting it away wet. Pour the water out, clean with lemon and salt, rinse until the water runs clear and dry before the next fill.</p>"
          }
        },
        "care_how_often": {
          "type": "accordion",
